{
  "abstract": "Architecture comparisons on this problem's shared harness are usually made at one budget (953 updates, 0.5B tokens), at equal wall time. We test whether such verdicts carry across budgets, running a 12-layer model and the 8-layer base at five budgets (953-2,773 updates, up to four seeds). At 953 updates 12L's equal-time lead is -2.4 ± 1.6 mBPB, a tie once the 2 mBPB timing band is included. At 1,906 updates it is -11.0 ± 1.3. On fitted curves 12L pulls ahead near 1,030 updates. Two registered tests confirm this without fitting: 12L at 1,906 updates beats base8 at 2,773 updates, the same wall time, by 8.85 and 8.20 mBPB, and 12L's per-update gain over base8 keeps growing to 2,773 updates. We fit a + b n^-c to each model and use the base curve as an equal-time scorer, anchored on the nearest measured run. Fits starting at 953 updates forecast runs 1.45 times longer within 0.4 mBPB for base8 and 0.8 for 12L. Fits on 238-953 updates miss by up to 3.6 mBPB and under-rate the deeper model. Timing, not fitting, dominates the error: base8's step time varies 6% across runs, moving every equal-time difference by about 2 mBPB, ten times the fit error. Same-seed pairing does not help: the seed sets only initialisation, and on three seeds its effects on base8 and 12L are anti-correlated (-0.85, -0.90). A wider MLP and an attention-path stack beat base8 at their own wall time, and a top-1 mixture of experts and a product-key memory tie it. Against 12L the last three lose and the wider MLP and a 16-layer model are within the timing band. All numbers recompute activations on every block; against an uncheckpointed base8 and a 12L recomputing five blocks, the estimated crossover moves to about 1,440 updates.",
